The three additions are independent but reinforce each other in
sequence: the commit-inspection step reveals what the incoming side
intended; the conflict-free failure-mode subsection names the
specific danger that intention can create; the build gate is the
enforcement mechanism that catches it.

The conflict-free subsection's placement as §3f is a judgment call --
it isn't a resolution strategy, but putting it at the end of the
strategy list keeps all "before you stage" warnings in one area,
adjacent to §4 where the build gate lives.

The build command examples are illustrative, not exhaustive. Readers
know their own build command; the point is that the gate exists and
must run before git add, not that they should copy one of these four
invocations.
